# Shell scripts are LF everywhere, including on a Windows checkout.
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf=true` is the default on Windows and on GitHub's Windows
# runners, so without this a clone there gets CRLF line endings — and `sh` reads
# the carriage return as part of the command, which fails as `$'\r': command not
# found` a long way from anything that looks like a line-ending problem.
#
# The scripts are run by `sh` on all three platforms (the baseline, the HTTP
# conformance checks, the LLVM install), so this is not cosmetic.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The same, for anything else read by a POSIX tool rather than by an editor.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Khora source is LF everywhere too, and this one is not cosmetic either.
#
# The formatter emits LF, and `the_corpus_is_already_formatted` compares its
# output against the file byte for byte. Without this a fresh clone on Windows
# gets CRLF, the comparison fails on every `.kh` file in `std`, and a new
# contributor's first `cargo test` fails on code they have not touched.
*.kh text eol=lf
